Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for industrial I/O (read/write holding registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RS-485/RS-232) Modbus RTU communication and does not natively support raw TCP connections 
    │   the way ModbusTcpClient does; a TCP-to-serial bridge or different transport setup would be required; minimalmodbus's Instrument class API 
    │   (read_register/write_register) differs significantly from pymodbus's response-object model (no isError(), different return semantics) -> requires a 
    │   connector/adapter layer; modbus-tk uses a TcpMaster + execute() command pattern instead of pymodbus's method-per-function-code style 
    │   (read_holding_registers/write_register), requiring rewriting of all I/O calls; Unit/slave ID handling differs across libraries (unit= kwarg in pymodbus
    │   vs slaveaddress in minimalmodbus vs positional args in modbus-tk); Error handling model changes: pymodbus returns response objects with isError(), 
    │   while minimalmodbus/modbus-tk typically raise exceptions directly
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three packages provide Modbus communication in Python and could theoretically replace pymodbus for reading/writing holding registers over TCP. The 
        file's own docstring explicitly calls out pymodbus -> minimalmodbus as a substitution target, acknowledging a different read/write API and error model.
        modbus-tk also implements a TCP master client with comparable register read/write semantics, making it a plausible functional substitute as well.

